(window.GROUP_QUIZZES=window.GROUP_QUIZZES||{})["clinical-medicine-and-surgery-i-exam-5-cms-exam-5-master-exam-form-c"]={"title":"CMS I Exam 5 Master Exam — Form C","category":"Clinical Medicine and Surgery I","exam":"Exam 5","sem":"fall-2026","questions":[{"q":"Which description fits recurrent pericarditis?","choices":["Symptoms persist without remission for up to 3 months","Symptoms return after a symptom-free gap of 4 to 6 weeks","Symptoms persist without any remission past 3 months","Constriction persists after an effusion has been drained"],"answer":1,"exp":"Correct. Recurrent pericarditis is when symptoms of acute pericarditis return after a symptom-free interval of 4 to 6 weeks or longer, which separates it from incessant disease. — 31. Pericardial Disease 2026.pptx, Slide 5"},{"q":"A 29-year-old woman who injects drugs has 5 days of high fever and rigors. Transthoracic imaging shows a large vegetation on the tricuspid valve, and three sets of blood cultures are drawn from separate sites. Which organism are the cultures most likely to grow?","choices":["Candida albicans","Viridans streptococci","Staphylococcus aureus","Enterococcus faecalis"],"answer":2,"exp":"Correct. In people who inject drugs, Staphylococcus aureus causes more than 60% of all endocarditis and 80-90% of cases where the tricuspid valve is infected. — 32. Infective Endocarditis FALL 2026 SV.pptx, Slide 18"},{"q":"A 44-year-old man with hypertrophic cardiomyopathy has chest pressure when climbing stairs. Coronary angiography performed before a planned myectomy shows normal coronary arteries. Which explanation best accounts for his exertional chest pain?","choices":["Unrecognized coronary plaque rupture","Outflow obstruction and thick muscle demand","A dilated, thin-walled ventricle","Inflammation of the pericardium"],"answer":1,"exp":"Correct. Angina without coronary disease in hypertrophic cardiomyopathy comes from left ventricular outflow tract obstruction and the increased oxygen consumption of hypertrophied muscle, causing ischemia during exertion. — Cardiomyopathy - Carter.pptx, Slide 66"},{"q":"A 69-year-old woman is brought in by her daughter for 2 days of new confusion. She has a fever of 38.9 °C (102.0 °F), a new murmur and conjunctival petechiae (pinpoint hemorrhages on the conjunctiva). Brain imaging shows multiple small scattered lesions without a large stroke. Which mechanism best explains her confusion?","choices":["A side effect of sedation","Septic cerebral emboli","A primary bacterial meningitis","Low-flow ischemia from heart block"],"answer":1,"exp":"Correct. Vegetations shower septic emboli into the systemic circulation; the brain is affected (ischemic stroke, abscess, hemorrhage, meningitis), producing neurologic deficits such as confusion. — 32. Infective Endocarditis FALL 2026 SV.pptx, Slide 11"},{"q":"Which organism is a fungal cause of pericarditis?","choices":["Coxsackievirus","Mycoplasma","Echinococcus","Histoplasma"],"answer":3,"exp":"Correct. The fungal causes listed are Histoplasma, Aspergillus, Blastomyces, Coccidioides and Candida; they are uncommon compared with idiopathic or viral disease. — 31. Pericardial Disease 2026.pptx, Slide 11"},{"q":"A 61-year-old man with a long QT has recurrent runs of torsades de pointes (QRS complexes that twist around the baseline). Between runs he is alert, with a pulse and a normal blood pressure. Which is the first treatment?","choices":["Intravenous magnesium","Intravenous procainamide","Intravenous amiodarone","Intravenous metoprolol"],"answer":0,"exp":"Correct. In a conscious patient, intravenous magnesium breaks torsades, followed by more magnesium as an infusion. — Arrhythmias II - Elwaya  (1).pptx, Slide 47"},{"q":"A 67-year-old woman with hypertension has an acute aortic dissection with severe tearing chest pain and a blood pressure of 176/100. Which intravenous medication should be started to lower her pressure?","choices":["Esmolol","Heparin","Cilostazol","Alteplase"],"answer":0,"exp":"Correct. Stopping progression of the dissection means reducing blood pressure, with systolic pressure brought to 100 to 120 mmHg using a beta blocker such as esmolol or a vasodilator such as nitroprusside. — Arterial Occlusive Diseaase and Aneurysms - Carter.pptx, Slides 51, 66, 67, 118"},{"q":"A 66-year-old man has a continuous bruit and a palpable thrill (a vibration felt over the skin) over the left femoral region, with mild leg swelling. He has not had dialysis and has no trauma history. His only recent procedure was an endovascular repair of an iliac artery performed through the left groin. Which is the most likely cause?","choices":["Progressive superficial venous reflux","Surgical creation for dialysis access","Groin access for the procedure","Congenital vascular malformation"],"answer":2,"exp":"Correct. Lower extremity arteriovenous fistulas are most commonly iatrogenic, caused by use of the groin femoral vessels for percutaneous arterial and venous access during endovascular procedures. — CMS I - Venous Disorders - Shah Fallsv UPDATED.pptx, Slide 82"},{"q":"What is the aim of cardiac rehabilitation programs in cardiomyopathy?","choices":["Screening of relatives for gene defects","Better exercise tolerance and quality of life","Replacement of all needed medications","Reversal of thickened ventricular walls"],"answer":1,"exp":"Correct. Cardiac rehabilitation programs aim to improve exercise tolerance and quality of life, with education on medications, nutrition and symptom monitoring and psychological support. — Cardiomyopathy - Carter.pptx, Slide 107"},{"q":"A genitourinary source of bacteremia points to which endocarditis organism?","choices":["Staphylococci","Haemophilus species","Enterococci","Viridans streptococci"],"answer":2,"exp":"Correct. Enterococci come from the genitourinary tract and are among the causes of native valve and subacute infective endocarditis. — 32. Infective Endocarditis FALL 2026 SV.pptx, Slide 17"},{"q":"A 55-year-old man has fatigue, exertional dyspnea, an elevated jugular venous pressure with the Kussmaul sign (a rise on inspiration), ascites and minimal ankle edema. His liver enzymes are mildly raised. Which condition is the key noncardiac mimic to consider?","choices":["Asthma","Hyperthyroidism","Pneumonia","Cirrhosis"],"answer":3,"exp":"Correct. Cirrhosis is in the differential of constrictive pericarditis because both cause ascites and fluid overload; an elevated jugular venous pressure with the Kussmaul sign points back to the heart. — 31. Pericardial Disease 2026.pptx, Slide 42"},{"q":"A 74-year-old woman has an asymptomatic abdominal aortic aneurysm measuring 5.3 cm on computed tomography. Which management is most appropriate?","choices":["Ultrasound every three years","Repair only once it ruptures","Elective repair","Imaging every 6 to 12 months"],"answer":2,"exp":"Correct. Elective repair is recommended at 5.0 cm or larger in women (over 5.5 cm in men). Women's aneurysms rupture more often, which is why their threshold is lower. — Arterial Occlusive Diseaase and Aneurysms - Carter.pptx, Slide 95"},{"q":"A 33-year-old man had a plaster cast removed from his right lower leg two days ago after a tibial fracture. He now has right calf swelling that measures 4 cm more than the left, and tenderness along the deep veins of the calf. Nothing else on the Wells criteria is present and no alternative diagnosis is as likely. What is his Wells score?","choices":["One point","Two points","Three points","Four points"],"answer":2,"exp":"Correct. Recent plaster immobilization of the lower extremity, calf swelling over 3 cm and tenderness along the deep venous system are worth 1 point each, for 3. A score of 3 or more means high likelihood, so ultrasound is ordered. — CMS I - Venous Disorders - Shah Fallsv UPDATED.pptx, Slide 55"},{"q":"A 38-year-old woman has her second recurrence of idiopathic pericarditis despite colchicine. Her temperature is 38.4 degrees Celsius, C-reactive protein is 45 mg/L, and cardiac magnetic resonance shows pericardial edema with late gadolinium enhancement. Which therapy is most appropriate to add?","choices":["Anakinra","Intravenous immunoglobulin","Azathioprine","Pericardiectomy"],"answer":0,"exp":"Correct. With an inflammatory phenotype (C-reactive protein above 10 mg/L, fever, imaging inflammation) and a colchicine-resistant second recurrence of idiopathic disease, an interleukin-1 blocker such as anakinra or rilonacept is added and now used earlier. — 31. Pericardial Disease 2026.pptx, Slide 35"},{"q":"A 45-year-old man presents with new-onset heart failure a week after a flulike illness. He smokes and has hyperlipidemia, so the team wants to exclude coronary disease before labeling this myocarditis. Which test rules out coronary ischemia as the cause of his new heart failure?","choices":["Chest radiograph","Sedimentation rate","Cardiac angiography","Endomyocardial biopsy"],"answer":2,"exp":"Correct. Cardiac angiography is used in the myocarditis work-up to rule out coronary ischemia as the cause of new-onset heart failure, which matters in a patient with coronary risk factors. — Cardiomyopathy - Carter.pptx, Slide 54"},{"q":"A 70-year-old man with chronic kidney disease has native valve endocarditis from penicillin-susceptible viridans streptococci. The team is choosing his antibiotic plan. Which choice should be avoided?","choices":["The 2-week gentamicin regimen","Monitoring renal function","Penicillin G for the full course","Ceftriaxone for the full course"],"answer":0,"exp":"Correct. The 2-week regimen with gentamicin is reserved for uncomplicated endocarditis with a rapid response and no chronic kidney disease; gentamicin is nephrotoxic, so he needs a standard course without it. — 32. Infective Endocarditis FALL 2026 SV.pptx, Slide 27"},{"q":"A 66-year-old man with a large anterior myocardial infarction two years ago has palpitations and lightheadedness. He is alert, with a blood pressure of 112/70. The monitor shows a regular wide QRS rhythm at 170 per minute with no P waves, every complex the same shape, for over a minute. Which is the most likely diagnosis?","choices":["Ventricular fibrillation","Monomorphic ventricular tachycardia","Polymorphic ventricular tachycardia","Complete heart block"],"answer":1,"exp":"Correct. A regular wide QRS rhythm of 100 to 250 per minute without P waves, with identical complexes lasting over 30 seconds, is sustained monomorphic ventricular tachycardia, often seen after myocardial infarction. — Arrhythmias II - Elwaya  (1).pptx, Slide 42"},{"q":"A 66-year-old woman with aortic valve endocarditis has a transesophageal echocardiogram that shows an abscess around the valve ring. Which is the most appropriate next step?","choices":["Repeat echo in 6 months","Oral step-down now","Shorten antibiotics","Surgical evaluation"],"answer":3,"exp":"Correct. Abscess is an indication for surgery, alongside heart failure from valve dysfunction, infection not resolving on appropriate antibiotics and fungal infection, so cardiothoracic surgery evaluates her. — 32. Infective Endocarditis FALL 2026 SV.pptx, Slide 33"},{"q":"A 58-year-old woman has Wenckebach block (a PR interval that grows longer until a beat is dropped) found on a routine tracing. She feels well and asks what would make her a candidate for a pacemaker. Which answer is most accurate?","choices":["Symptoms such as fainting","Dropped beats during sleep","A PR above 0.20 seconds","Being over 65 years old"],"answer":0,"exp":"Correct. Mobitz type I needs no treatment unless symptoms are debilitating; a permanent pacemaker is considered for symptomatic patients, such as those with dizziness, exertional intolerance or syncope. — Arrhythmias II - Elwaya  (1).pptx, Slide 9"},{"q":"In unstable Mobitz type II block, when medications are not used or do not work, which is the emergent measure?","choices":["Synchronized cardioversion","Vagal maneuvers","Defibrillation","Transcutaneous pacing"],"answer":3,"exp":"Correct. Transcutaneous pacing, with pads on the chest, is the emergent measure for unstable Mobitz type II block; it bridges the patient to a permanent pacemaker. — Arrhythmias II - Elwaya  (1).pptx, Slide 14"},{"q":"Stable atrial fibrillation with rapid ventricular response is treated how?","choices":["Intravenous rate control","Synchronized cardioversion","Immediate ablation","Intravenous atropine"],"answer":0,"exp":"Correct. A hemodynamically stable patient in rapid ventricular response gets intravenous rate control; a stable patient without it gets rate control by mouth. — arrhythmias I - Elwaya-2.pptx, Slide 49"},{"q":"A 64-year-old man in atrial flutter has a heart rate of 150, a blood pressure of 70/40, and is confused. Which is the next step?","choices":["Oral rate control","Anticoagulate for 21 days","Vagal maneuver","Synchronized cardioversion"],"answer":3,"exp":"Correct. Hemodynamically unstable atrial flutter (hypotension with confusion) is treated with synchronized cardioversion. — arrhythmias I - Elwaya-2.pptx, Slide 45"},{"q":"A 71-year-old woman admitted with stress cardiomyopathy after a house fire has a repeat echocardiogram that shows a thrombus in the akinetic left ventricular apex. She is in sinus rhythm. Which treatment is now indicated?","choices":["Nitrate infusion","Pacemaker insertion","Anticoagulation","Surgical myectomy"],"answer":2,"exp":"Correct. Treatment of Takotsubo is empiric and supportive and depends on the presentation: anticoagulation if a left ventricular thrombus is present, beta blockers as helpful, and diuretics if heart failure is present. — Cardiomyopathy - Carter.pptx, Slide 104"},{"q":"A 26-year-old woman had a peripherally inserted central catheter placed in her right arm two weeks ago for long-term antibiotic therapy. She reports aching and swelling of the arm. Examination shows diffuse swelling of the entire right upper arm with mild redness, and there is no palpable cord and no purulent drainage. Which is the most appropriate next step?","choices":["Venous duplex ultrasound of the arm","Warm compresses and ibuprofen","Oral antibiotics for cellulitis","Reassurance and arm elevation"],"answer":0,"exp":"Correct. Upper extremity deep vein thrombosis often presents only with limb swelling and arm discomfort, and indwelling central venous catheters are a leading cause. Duplex ultrasound of the involved limb is the test. — CMS I - Venous Disorders - Shah Fallsv UPDATED.pptx, Slide 51"},{"q":"A 45-year-old woman with varicose veins has a small, tender, red cord along the great saphenous vein of the calf. Venous duplex ultrasound confirms a short segment of superficial clot. She has no other risk factors for thrombosis. Which is the most appropriate initial treatment?","choices":["Inferior vena cava filter placement","Broad-spectrum intravenous antibiotics","Warm compresses and ibuprofen","Catheter-directed thrombolysis"],"answer":2,"exp":"Correct. Typical treatment is warm compresses, compression, elevation of the limb and an anti-inflammatory drug such as ibuprofen or diclofenac for pain and inflammation. Anticoagulation is reserved for higher risk. — CMS I - Venous Disorders - Shah Fallsv UPDATED.pptx, Slide 79"},{"q":"A 33-year-old man has a regular narrow complex tachycardia at 190 with hidden P waves. He is alert with a blood pressure of 128/80. A properly performed vagal maneuver does not change the rhythm. Which is the next step?","choices":["Intravenous magnesium","Adenosine","Atropine","Synchronized cardioversion"],"answer":1,"exp":"Correct. In stable supraventricular tachycardia, adenosine follows a failed vagal maneuver and may be repeated once; it may convert the rhythm or slow it enough to show P waves. — arrhythmias I - Elwaya-2.pptx, Slide 26"},{"q":"A 67-year-old woman is recovering in hospital from stress cardiomyopathy that followed news of her son's serious illness. Her chest pain has resolved, and she asks whether her heart will stay damaged. Which answer about her prognosis is most accurate?","choices":["Most patients recover completely","It always progresses to failure","Poorest outlook of any type","Half die within five years"],"answer":0,"exp":"Correct. Takotsubo is a transient regional systolic dysfunction, and most patients completely recover, which distinguishes its prognosis from the progressive cardiomyopathies. — Cardiomyopathy - Carter.pptx, Slide 104"},{"q":"A 31-year-old woman has inappropriate sinus tachycardia at about 110 after a full workup excluded other causes. Her only symptom is awareness of her heartbeat, and she asks whether she needs medication. Which response is most accurate?","choices":["She needs a lifelong beta blocker","She needs anticoagulation","Usually left untreated; benign","Cardioversion will cure it"],"answer":2,"exp":"Correct. Inappropriate sinus tachycardia is generally left untreated: patients tolerate the symptoms better than the treatment, beta blockers are poorly tolerated, and the prognosis is usually benign. — arrhythmias I - Elwaya-2.pptx, Slide 17"},{"q":"A 62-year-old woman with suspected tamponade undergoes right heart catheterization before drainage. Which pressure finding is expected?","choices":["An isolated high left atrial pressure","Low right atrial pressure","Equal pressures in all chambers","A gradient across the aortic valve"],"answer":2,"exp":"Correct. Cardiac catheterization in tamponade shows equalization of pressures in all chambers of the heart, because the pericardial fluid under pressure dictates diastolic pressure throughout. — 31. Pericardial Disease 2026.pptx, Slide 71"},{"q":"A 76-year-old woman has episodes of lightheadedness and one faint. A 24-hour ambulatory monitor shows runs of a fast, irregular rhythm that alternate with slow rates and long pauses. Which fast rhythm is most likely on her monitor?","choices":["Atrioventricular nodal reentry","Atrial fibrillation","Sinus tachycardia","Ventricular fibrillation"],"answer":1,"exp":"Correct. Fast rhythms alternating with bradycardia and offset pauses are tachy-brady syndrome, a form of sick sinus syndrome in which the tachyarrhythmia is most commonly atrial fibrillation. — arrhythmias I - Elwaya-2.pptx, Slide 18"},{"q":"Why is renal function checked in peripheral artery disease?","choices":["To measure homocysteine","Risk of kidney stones","To grade the claudication","Risk of renal insufficiency"],"answer":3,"exp":"Correct. Patients with peripheral artery disease are at risk for renal insufficiency, so renal function joins the electrocardiogram, blood count, lipid profile, metabolic panel and hemoglobin A1c. — Arterial Occlusive Diseaase and Aneurysms - Carter.pptx, Slide 49"},{"q":"A 72-year-old man with long-standing hypertension has a routine electrocardiogram. The QRS is 0.16 seconds wide, with a dominant S wave in V1, a broad single-peaked R wave in leads I, V5 and V6, and no Q waves in those leads. Which is the most likely diagnosis?","choices":["Left bundle branch block","Right bundle branch block","Ventricular tachycardia","First-degree block"],"answer":0,"exp":"Correct. A QRS over 0.12 seconds with a dominant S wave in V1, a monophasic R in the lateral leads and absent Q waves in I, V5 and V6 is left bundle branch block; chronic hypertension is a named cause. — Arrhythmias II - Elwaya  (1).pptx, Slide 24"},{"q":"A 41-year-old woman with dilated cardiomyopathy also has enlarged hilar lymph nodes and skin nodules, and an infiltrative process such as sarcoidosis is suspected. Coronary angiography showed no disease. Which imaging test best identifies an inflammatory or infiltrative cause?","choices":["Ambulatory rhythm monitoring for forty-eight hours","Cardiac magnetic resonance imaging with gadolinium","A repeat resting electrocardiogram in six months","Cardiac catheterization with pressure tracings"],"answer":1,"exp":"Correct. Cardiac magnetic resonance imaging with gadolinium contrast helps identify inflammatory, ischemic or infiltrative processes such as sarcoidosis or hemochromatosis in dilated cardiomyopathy. — Cardiomyopathy - Carter.pptx, Slide 41"},{"q":"A 24-year-old man whose Wolff-Parkinson-White was found on a school electrocardiogram presents with palpitations. The rhythm is irregular and wide-complex, at rates up to 260, with varying delta waves (slurred upstrokes). He is alert with a blood pressure of 118/74. Which drug is the most appropriate treatment?","choices":["Intravenous procainamide","Atropine","Intravenous magnesium","Adenosine"],"answer":0,"exp":"Correct. Irregular, wide, very fast complexes in a patient with Wolff-Parkinson-White are pre-excited atrial fibrillation, treated with intravenous procainamide; adenosine must never be used. — arrhythmias I - Elwaya-2.pptx, Slide 37"},{"q":"A 70-year-old man had 20 minutes of weakness and numbness in his right hand four months ago that fully resolved. Duplex ultrasonography shows 75% stenosis of the left internal carotid artery. A friend with no symptoms was told surgery waits until stenosis passes 80%, and he asks whether the same applies to him. Which response is accurate?","choices":["Yes; resolved symptoms do not count","No; over 70% already qualifies him","Yes; surgery waits until over 80%","No; he needs medical therapy only"],"answer":1,"exp":"Correct. A transient ischemic attack (focal loss of neurologic function lasting under 24 hours) within the last six months makes him symptomatic, and surgery is advised for symptomatic stenosis over 70%; the over-80% rule is for asymptomatic patients. — Arterial Occlusive Diseaase and Aneurysms - Carter.pptx, Slides 72, 75"},{"q":"A 50-year-old man with acute pericarditis says the pain keeps him awake because it is worse when he lies down. Which advice is most appropriate?","choices":["Keep the head of the bed elevated","Sleep prone to splint the chest","Sleep flat to rest the heart","Take an extra dose at bedtime"],"answer":0,"exp":"Correct. Pericardial pain is worse supine and better leaning forward, so keeping the head of the bed elevated is advised, along with completing the full treatment course. — 31. Pericardial Disease 2026.pptx, Slide 33"},{"q":"Where do atherosclerotic lesions most often form?","choices":["In the smallest arterioles of the skin","In straight segments with laminar flow","In the veins draining the lower legs","At branch points and turbulent sites"],"answer":3,"exp":"Correct. Atherosclerotic lesions form at arterial bifurcations (branching) and at sites of increased turbulence, and they develop over many decades before causing stenosis, thrombosis or atheroemboli. — Arterial Occlusive Diseaase and Aneurysms - Carter.pptx, Slide 7"},{"q":"When are palliative care discussions raised for elderly patients with cardiomyopathy?","choices":["At the first visit","Never in the elderly","After transplant only","In advanced stages"],"answer":3,"exp":"Correct. Palliative care discussions belong to the advanced stages in the elderly, alongside tailored therapy, cautious diuretics and vasodilators, and a focus on quality of life. — Cardiomyopathy - Carter.pptx, Slide 106"},{"q":"A 76-year-old woman has constant throbbing, burning pain in her toes and forefoot that is worse lying flat, not relieved by narcotic pain medicine, and eased by dangling her leg or walking around. Her feet are cool. Which is the most likely diagnosis?","choices":["Intermittent claudication","Peripheral neuropathy","Acute limb ischemia","Critical limb ischemia"],"answer":3,"exp":"Correct. Ischemic rest pain of critical limb ischemia is constant, distal, worse with elevation or reclining, poorly controlled even by narcotics, and relieved by dependency or walking, which use gravity to aid perfusion. — Arterial Occlusive Diseaase and Aneurysms - Carter.pptx, Slides 19, 21, 23, 40, 59"},{"q":"Which acute event can cause right bundle branch block by suddenly raising right ventricular pressure?","choices":["Hypertensive crisis","Aortic dissection","Pulmonary embolism","Acute gastroenteritis"],"answer":2,"exp":"Correct. A sudden increase in right ventricular pressure, as from a pulmonary embolism, is a named cause of right bundle branch block. — Arrhythmias II - Elwaya  (1).pptx, Slide 31"},{"q":"A 69-year-old woman with right-sided heart failure, ascites and a raised jugular venous pressure has cardiac magnetic resonance imaging to decide between restrictive cardiomyopathy and constrictive pericarditis. Which imaging finding would point to constrictive pericarditis instead?","choices":["Preserved ejection fraction","Normal ventricular cavity size","Pericardial thickening","Marked atrial enlargement"],"answer":2,"exp":"Correct. Cardiac magnetic resonance imaging helps separate the two conditions, and a thickened pericardium points to constrictive pericarditis rather than restrictive cardiomyopathy, though constriction can occur with a normal-thickness pericardium. — Cardiomyopathy - Carter.pptx, Slide 96"},{"q":"A 58-year-old woman with previous infective endocarditis needs a root canal. She developed hives (urticaria) after amoxicillin years ago and can take pills. Which prophylaxis is most appropriate?","choices":["Oral amoxicillin","Oral cephalexin","Oral azithromycin","Oral clindamycin"],"answer":2,"exp":"Correct. A penicillin-allergic patient who can take oral medication gets azithromycin or clarithromycin, or doxycycline; cephalexin is avoided after urticaria to penicillin. — 32. Infective Endocarditis FALL 2026 SV.pptx, Slide 35"},{"q":"A 66-year-old woman in torsades de pointes (a polymorphic rhythm whose QRS complexes twist around the baseline) becomes unresponsive with a blood pressure of 60/30 and only a faint pulse. Which is the most appropriate next step?","choices":["Defibrillation","Intravenous lidocaine","Transcutaneous pacing","Synchronized cardioversion"],"answer":0,"exp":"Correct. A hemodynamically unstable patient in torsades de pointes is defibrillated (unsynchronized cardioversion); polymorphic rhythms are not synchronized. — Arrhythmias II - Elwaya  (1).pptx, Slide 47"},{"q":"A 68-year-old man has had 6 weeks of low-grade fever and weight loss and has a new murmur. Subacute endocarditis is suspected. Which complete blood count finding is most likely?","choices":["Eosinophilia","Normocytic anemia","Microcytic anemia","Polycythemia"],"answer":1,"exp":"Correct. The complete blood count often shows leukocytosis and anemia of chronic disease with a normal mean corpuscular volume (normocytic). — 32. Infective Endocarditis FALL 2026 SV.pptx, Slide 25"},{"q":"A 65-year-old woman with a normal pulse examination and ankle-brachial index has had her venous ulcer heal after months of compression. She asks why it opened again after she bumped her leg. Which feature of a healed venous ulcer explains this?","choices":["Fragile thin scar tissue","Undertreated deep infection","Lymphatic channel obstruction","Persistent arterial ischemia"],"answer":0,"exp":"Correct. A venous ulcer heals with a thin scar that breaks down easily with minor trauma or increased leg edema, so recurrence is common without consistent compression. — CMS I - Venous Disorders - Shah Fallsv UPDATED.pptx, Slide 15"},{"q":"A 30-year-old woman with known atrioventricular nodal reentry has a regular narrow complex tachycardia at 178. She is stable. Which treatment almost always terminates it?","choices":["Synchronized cardioversion","Catheter ablation","Intravenous procainamide","Vagal maneuvers or adenosine"],"answer":3,"exp":"Correct. Atrioventricular nodal reentry almost always terminates with vagal maneuvers or adenosine; synchronized cardioversion is rarely required. — arrhythmias I - Elwaya-2.pptx, Slide 41"},{"q":"A 54-year-old man has a moderate-to-large pericardial effusion on echocardiogram with no hemodynamic compromise. Viral, uremic, thyroid and rheumatologic workups are unrevealing. Which is the most appropriate next step?","choices":["Start empiric anakinra","Immediate pericardiectomy","Diagnostic pericardiocentesis","Reassure and discharge"],"answer":2,"exp":"Correct. Diagnostic pericardiocentesis is considered for a moderate-to-large effusion of unknown etiology, as well as during therapeutic drainage for tamponade and with suspected neoplastic, purulent or tuberculous disease. — 31. Pericardial Disease 2026.pptx, Slide 67"},{"q":"A 72-year-old man with extensive atherosclerosis develops painful, tender, bluish discoloration of several toes on his left foot, with localized pallor. His dorsalis pedis and posterior tibial pulses are easily palpable. Which is the most likely diagnosis?","choices":["Atheroembolism","Acute embolic limb ischemia","Dry gangrene","Critical limb ischemia"],"answer":0,"exp":"Correct. Atheroemboli of fibrin, platelets and cholesterol debris from proximal plaques lodge in small vessels of muscle and skin, causing blue toe syndrome with tenderness and pallor while distal pulses usually stay palpable. — Arterial Occlusive Diseaase and Aneurysms - Carter.pptx, Slide 68"},{"q":"A 23-year-old previously healthy man has breathlessness, chest pain and exercise intolerance ten days after a flulike illness with fever, myalgia and diarrhea. He is tachycardic with a third heart sound (an early diastolic sound just after the second heart sound) and crackles at both bases. Troponin is elevated and coronary angiography is normal. Which is the most likely diagnosis?","choices":["Takotsubo cardiomyopathy","Acute viral myocarditis","Restrictive cardiomyopathy","Hypertrophic cardiomyopathy"],"answer":1,"exp":"Correct. Acute heart failure in an otherwise healthy young man with no cardiac history, one to two weeks after a flulike prodrome, with raised troponin and normal coronaries, is acute viral myocarditis. — Cardiomyopathy - Carter.pptx, Slide 51"},{"q":"A 71-year-old man with severe chronic obstructive pulmonary disease is admitted with an exacerbation and respiratory failure. His pulse is 128 and irregular. The electrocardiogram shows a P wave before every QRS, with at least three different P-wave shapes in lead II. Which is the most likely diagnosis?","choices":["Atrial fibrillation","Multifocal atrial tachycardia","Atrial flutter, variable block","Focal atrial tachycardia"],"answer":1,"exp":"Correct. An irregular tachycardia with a P wave for every QRS and at least three distinct P-wave shapes in one lead is multifocal atrial tachycardia, usually from severe lung disease or heart failure in an acutely ill patient. — arrhythmias I - Elwaya-2.pptx, Slide 28"},{"q":"A 28-year-old woman with a malar rash, painful joints and a positive antinuclear antibody test has a new murmur. She has no fever, repeated blood cultures are negative, and echocardiography shows small sterile-appearing valve vegetations. Which is the most likely cause of her valve lesion?","choices":["Subacute infective endocarditis","Device endocarditis","Acute infective endocarditis","Libman-Sacks endocarditis"],"answer":3,"exp":"Correct. Systemic lupus erythematosus may present with non-infective (Libman-Sacks) endocarditis, which mimics infective endocarditis in the autoimmune differential. — 32. Infective Endocarditis FALL 2026 SV.pptx, Slide 21"},{"q":"A 57-year-old woman in ventricular fibrillation arrest has received shocks, epinephrine and amiodarone, and she remains in ventricular fibrillation. Which drug may be considered next?","choices":["Metoprolol","Adenosine","Lidocaine","Verapamil"],"answer":2,"exp":"Correct. Lidocaine is considered for ventricular fibrillation that remains refractory to shocks after amiodarone. — Arrhythmias II - Elwaya  (1).pptx, Slide 50"},{"q":"A 66-year-old man with a known ascending thoracic aortic aneurysm develops a new diastolic murmur, exertional dyspnea and leg swelling. Which complication does the aneurysm's location explain?","choices":["Esophageal compression","Aortic regurgitation","Tracheal compression","Recurrent laryngeal palsy"],"answer":1,"exp":"Correct. An ascending thoracic aneurysm can cause aortic regurgitation and heart failure, which explains the new diastolic murmur, breathlessness and edema. — Arterial Occlusive Diseaase and Aneurysms - Carter.pptx, Slide 104"},{"q":"In the low likelihood Wells category, what happens after a negative D-dimer?","choices":["Duplex ultrasound follows","Venography is performed","The test is repeated in a week","The work-up stops"],"answer":3,"exp":"Correct. In the low likelihood Wells category a negative D-dimer ends the work-up, since the test is sensitive enough to rule out deep vein thrombosis. — CMS I - Venous Disorders - Shah Fallsv UPDATED.pptx, Slide 56"},{"q":"A 52-year-old man with a pacemaker implanted two weeks ago has three days of swelling and aching of the left arm. The pacemaker lead has injured the wall of the subclavian vein. Which component of the Virchow triad does this injury represent?","choices":["Hypercoagulable state","Endothelial trauma","Venous stasis","Impaired lymphatic drainage"],"answer":1,"exp":"Correct. Damage to the vessel lining from an indwelling device is endothelial trauma, one leg of the Virchow triad. Pacemakers, implanted defibrillators and central venous catheters are the most common causes of upper-extremity deep vein thrombosis. — CMS I - Venous Disorders - Shah Fallsv UPDATED.pptx, Slide 47"},{"q":"A 37-year-old man was treated for acute pericarditis 4 weeks ago and now feels well. A repeat electrocardiogram shows widespread T-wave inversions without ST elevation, and troponin is normal. Which best explains the tracing?","choices":["Expected evolution of the illness","A new silent myocardial infarction","Developing cardiac tamponade","Hyperkalemia from colchicine"],"answer":0,"exp":"Correct. The classic evolution is widespread ST elevation with PR depression, then ST normalization with T-wave flattening, then inversion of the flattened T waves, and finally a normal tracing. — 31. Pericardial Disease 2026.pptx, Slide 22"},{"q":"A 71-year-old man admitted with pneumonia develops frequent early, wide QRS beats with no preceding P wave on telemetry, and he feels his heart skipping. His oxygen saturation on the monitor is drifting down. Which is the most appropriate next step?","choices":["Start intravenous amiodarone","Immediate defibrillation","Refer for catheter ablation","Check blood gas, electrolytes"],"answer":3,"exp":"Correct. Hypoxemia and electrolyte imbalance are the main causes of premature ventricular contractions, so new frequent beats call for checking oxygenation and electrolytes first. — Arrhythmias II - Elwaya  (1).pptx, Slide 36"},{"q":"An 80-year-old man is found on the floor two days after collapsing. His right leg has profound sensory loss, paralysis with rigid muscles, and no arterial or venous Doppler signal. Which is the most appropriate next step?","choices":["Urgent computed tomography angiography","Catheter-directed thrombolysis","Surgical thromboembolectomy","Major amputation"],"answer":3,"exp":"Correct. An irreversibly ischemic limb has major tissue loss or permanent nerve damage, profound sensory loss, paralysis and inaudible arterial and venous signals; nonviable limbs require major amputation whatever therapy is used. — Arterial Occlusive Diseaase and Aneurysms - Carter.pptx, Slide 64"},{"q":"What is the first-line treatment for acute, symptomatic, hemodynamically unstable bradycardia?","choices":["A permanent pacemaker","Adenosine","Amiodarone","Atropine"],"answer":3,"exp":"Correct. Atropine is first line for acute, symptomatic, hemodynamically unstable bradycardia; if it fails, a temporary pacemaker follows, and chronic symptomatic disease gets a permanent pacemaker. — arrhythmias I - Elwaya-2.pptx, Slide 13"},{"q":"A 49-year-old man has a first proximal deep vein thrombosis with no surgery, trauma, hormone use, travel, cancer or other identifiable trigger, and no inherited disorder has been found. How long should anticoagulation continue?","choices":["Three to six months","Indefinitely","Six weeks","Three months"],"answer":1,"exp":"Correct. A first venous thromboembolism with no identifiable risk factor is unprovoked, and its source, such as an undiscovered inherited clotting disorder, is unknown. Treatment therefore continues for an indefinite length of time. — CMS I - Venous Disorders - Shah Fallsv UPDATED.pptx, Slide 64"}]};
